21. The primary lesion exhibits a large ulcer, conferring a high risk of hemorrhage or perforation; alternatively, imaging studies (CT or MRI), with or without adjunctive gastroscopy, demonstrate transmural tumor invasion-i.e., infiltration across the full thickness of the gastric wall-thereby elevating the patient's risk of hemorrhage or perforation;
22. Patients present with unstable or active peptic ulcers, active gastrointestinal bleeding, or a history of major gastrointestinal bleeding within the preceding three months;
23. Concurrent enrollment in another clinical study, unless it is an observational (non-interventional) clinical study;
